      "css": ".ds-sheet{position:relative;background:#ecdcae;color:#2a2416;border-radius:8px;padding:28px 18px 26px;box-shadow:0 18px 30px -14px rgba(4,16,26,.65);font-family:system-ui,sans-serif}.ds-sheet::before,.ds-sheet::after{content:'';position:absolute;top:-7px;width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:50%;background:radial-gradient(circle at 40% 35%,#eef2f4,#7d8a94 70%);box-shadow:0 2px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.45)}.ds-sheet::before{left:24px}.ds-sheet::after{right:24px}.ds-sheet h2{margin:0 0 12px;font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:-.02em;font-size:clamp(32px,9vw,56px);line-height:.98}.ds-sheet p{margin:0;color:#5c4f30;font-size:17px;line-height:1.55}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Pegboard Wall",
    "overview": "The page is the back wall of the shop: painted steel-blue pegboard on a strict 24px hole grid, with real door hardware (torsion springs, extension springs, rollers, hinges, lift cables, winding bars) hanging from peg hooks, manila parts tags strung on pegs, and a manila work order pinned up for the estimate. Everything that hangs obeys gravity and can be pushed. One color, emergency-release red, is reserved for the action that gets a homeowner's stuck door fixed: text or call.\n\nThe system is phone-first. It is judged at 360 to 430px inside Facebook's in-app browser; wider layouts are the phone layout given room (two columns, a wider rack), never a separate design. Type is a heavy uppercase system sans set tight and large, so the page reads like stencilled shop signage and loads with zero font requests. Density is low: one idea per section, big tap targets (44px minimum, 56px for primary actions), generous section padding.\n\nMotion is physical, never decorative easing. Every moving thing is a damped spring or pendulum driven from one shared animation loop that sleeps when nothing is on screen, and every moving thing renders a still frame under prefers-reduced-motion. The category's stock photo of a smiling tech beside a van is the confirmed anti-reference; the hardware itself is the imagery.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Painted pegboard ground with a real 24px hole grid; hardware snaps to the holes.",
      "Manila (tags, work order) is the only light surface; it carries all reading-heavy and form content that is not a headline.",
      "Emergency red appears only on primary actions and their hover/active echoes.",
      "System sans at weight 800 to 900, uppercase for display and section headlines.",
      "Pendulum and spring physics, pointer-driven, with still frames for reduced motion."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Handle Rule",
        "body": "Emergency red marks the action that gets help (text or call) and nothing else. No red headings, no red decoration on the page surface; inside the rack it appears only where real hardware is red.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Materials Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces are either painted board (blue family) or manila paper. There is no white card, no grey panel, no third material.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Shop Lettering Rule",
        "body": "Headlines are weight 900, uppercase, negative tracking (-0.02 to -0.025em), line-height under 1. Body never goes below 15px; phone-first body is 17px.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Number Rule",
        "body": "Every phone number (header, closing tag, footer) uses tabular figures and never wraps.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hole Grid Rule",
        "body": "The pegboard grid is 24px (`--grid`). Hanging hardware snaps its hook to a hole centre; anything that hangs on the board lands on the grid. In the hero, the hole pattern is masked out behind the copy (measured live as `--copy-h` / `--copy-w`) so text never sits on holes.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Seam Rule",
